          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "An underactive thyroid, also known as hypothyroidism, means your thyroid gland isn't producing enough thyroid hormones.\n\nIt's typically recognized by a raised level of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) in your blood. If your free thyroxine level is also low, it's called \"overt hypothyroidism.\" If your free thyroxine level is normal despite a raised TSH, it's called \"subclinical hypothyroidism.\" (Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, General clinical reference)\n\nThe general implications are related to its symptoms, which can develop slowly and are often non-specific. These include fatigue, feeling cold, weight gain, constipation, dry skin, thinning hair, muscle aches, heavy or irregular periods, low mood, and slowed thinking. Because these symptoms are common in many conditions, it can be challenging to diagnose hypothyroidism based on symptoms alone. It's important to confirm the condition with repeated blood tests rather than just treating symptoms or a single test result. (General clinical reference)\n\nThe information available does not describe the typical management of an underactive thyroid beyond diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
